  "text": "Let's first determine the intersection point between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the circle $x^2+y^2-4x-4y-5=0$. \n\n1. To find the intersection points of the parabola and the circle, we need to equate the two equations. That gives us:\n$$(x^2)^2 + x^2 - 4x - 4x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0$$\n2. Simplifying the equation, we get:\n$$x^4 - 3x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0$$\n3. We can solve this polynomial equation using numerical methods (e.g. Newton-Raphson method or Bisection method).\n\nAfter solving, we'll get the value of x. We can then plug this value back into either the parabola equation $y = x^2$ or the circle equation to get the corresponding y-coordinate.\n\n4. Once we have the coordinates of the intersection point, we can determine the tangent line by using the implicit function theorem. Here, we'll use the chain rule to find the derivative of the circle equation with respect to x.\n\nThe implicit function theorem can be stated as follows:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}}{\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}}$$\nwhere $g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 4x - 4y - 5 = 0$ and $f(x,y) = x^2 - y = 0$. \n\nAfter we determine the derivative of the circle equation with respect to x, we can plug the coordinates of the intersection point into this derivative to get the slope of the tangent line. \n\n5. Finally, we can use the point-slope form of a line equation to obtain the equation of the tangent line:\n$$y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$$\nwhere $m$ is the slope of the tangent line and $(x_1, y_1)$ is the point where the line touches the parabola or circle. \n\nAfter performing these calculations, we'll find the equation of the tangent line to the parabola at the intersection point.\n\n닪acomment\nThe intersection point is $(1,1)$ and the tangent line equation is $x-y=0$.",
